<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narathihapate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1238">1238</a>–<a href="/years/1287">1287</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Tayokpyemin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narathihapate was king of Pagan from 1256 to 1287. His refusal of the tribute demanded by the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> brought defeat at Ngasaunggyan in 1277 and the loss of the north in the 1280s. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, earning the name Tayokpyemin, 'the king who fled from the Chinese', and was murdered by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a> at Prome in 1287, after which the kingdom broke apart.</p></div>
